        "thinking": "I see — `expressHandler` tags the wrapper with `__handler = handler`. The `handler` is the transport-agnostic Handler function. The `deps` (db, storage) are passed to `expressHandler` but I need to see how they're used.\n\nLooking at the `wrapper` function — it uses `deps.db` and `deps.storage` to build the `RequestCtx`. So the handler captures `deps` which includes `db`. The db is captured at route-building time.\n\nNow, the express-router-bridge extracts `rl.handle.__handler` which is the `handler` (not `deps`). \n\nSo the key insight is that `__handler` points to the transport-agnostic handler function itself, not the wrapper that closes over `deps`. The wrapper is what actually gets invoked by Express, and it's the wrapper that constructs the `RequestCtx` using the captured `deps` before passing it to the handler. So the handlers receive `db` from `ctx.db` at request time, not from closure — and that `db` comes from the cached route object created during initialization, which means every request is using the same stale database connection.\n\nThe solution is to stop caching `db` and `storage` in the route cache and instead create fresh instances per request. The tricky part is that the route factories need `db` and `storage` to build the router structure, but since the actual handlers get those from `ctx` anyway, I could pass stub objects just to construct the routes, then swap in real per-request instances when handling requests.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
    ],
